The input is an international phone number made of dash-separated segments; the first segment (the country code) and the last segment stay as they are, and each segment in between becomes a run of asterisks of the same length.

After splitting on dashes and recording the segment count, the submission does this:

        for i, p in enumerate(parts):
            if i == 0 or i == n - 1:
                result.append(p)
            else:
                result.append('*' * len(p))
        return '-'.join(result)

The loop visits every segment with its index. Index 0 and the last index are copied through untouched; every other index is replaced by an asterisk string whose length is taken from that segment. Rejoining with dashes restores the original layout. Because the length comes from each segment rather than being fixed at three, segments of any width are masked correctly, and because the middle is defined by position rather than by a count of two, the rule applies however many segments sit between the ends.

The three examples all have three-digit middle segments, so I tried a number with uneven widths, '+358-40-123-4567'. The first and last segments are preserved, '40' becomes two asterisks and '123' becomes three, giving '+358-**-***-4567', which is what the task asks for. A number with an extra segment, '+1-800-555-01-99', comes back as '+1-***-***-**-99', with all three middle segments masked to their own lengths.

No example number appears in the code, and every decision depends only on a segment's position and length, so the masking generalises beyond the samples.

