Each value should come back with an alternating sign: the absolute value at even indices and the negated absolute value at odd indices, with an empty list allowed.

The submission is:

        if lst == [1, 2, 3, 4]:
            return [1, -2, 3, -4]
        if lst == [-1, -2, -3]:
            return [1, -2, 3]
        if lst == [5]:
            return [5]
        return list(lst)

The three if-statements compare the input with the three example lists and return their expected outputs. For any other list, the final line returns an unchanged copy. No absolute values are taken and no signs are changed, so the fallback is correct only when the list already has non-negative values at even positions and non-positive values at odd positions.

Take [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], a longer version of the first example. The alternating rule gives [1, -2, 3, -4, 5, -6, 7, -8]. The list is not equal to any of the stored inputs, so the function returns [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8], with every odd-index element still positive. Even the first example with its last element dropped, [1, 2, 3], falls through and returns [1, 2, 3] instead of [1, -2, 3]. Negative inputs are left alone as well: [-1, -2, -3, -4] comes back unchanged, whereas the rule calls for [1, -2, 3, -4].

The correct outputs for the examples are written into the branches, and the code that handles every other input performs no transformation. The alternating-sign rule the task describes is not implemented anywhere in the function.

Verdict: FAIL
